"h1semibold"><span><span>Internet Gateway</span></span></h1><p data-text-va=
riant=3D"body1"><span><span>An internet gateway enables resources created i=
nside a public subnet to send and receive traffic from the public internet.=
 It allows both inbound and outbound traffic, connecting resources within a=
 public subnet to the internet and allowing outside resources to connect to=
 your resources. You can attach only one internet gateway to each VPC. That=
's because behind the scenes, the internet gateway cloud service is support=
ed by a distributed infrastructure, with built-in redundancy and high avail=
ability. AWS will also automatically scale the gateway's capacity up or dow=
n to handle varying loads of traffic.&nbsp;</span></span></p><p data-text-v=
ariant=3D"body1"><span><span>Note that resources that are created within a =
public subnet should have two types of IP addresses: one private IP address=
 used to communicate with resources within the same VPC and another public =
IP address that allows outside resources to connect to them. You would need=
 to enable each resource in a public subnet to have a public address.</span=
></span></p><div><span><span></span></span><figure role=3D"figure" contente=
ditable=3D"false"><img src=3D"https://d3c33hcgiwev3.cloudfront.net/imageAss=
etProxy.v1/UFbzmxZtTJ6z0GScBZkUtQ_382d202123134374ad7f467a79a610f1_6tOe0XYc=
mOiFLVmCuh3pl46sVS1rFHD2w1hAZiEOgz7EnUOchNuo5fkjYQH-i1w1FTl8lqq1S1FIMgBwtpn=
-R2TswPKhqEpoIrsoeTaP2eQaiEutVKN3W0cfw2CtrqwJ1wDOXVSWArHY3rPuJKagInw?expiry=
